## Narrative
The report is a real protocol flaw in the authenticated storage RPC runtime. Response signing serializes only the response frame plus target/topology metadata and uses request_id as the envelope sequence; the verifier receives only expected target/topology/kind/request_id/time/envelope and validates those fields without any original request payload or digest. The activated Unix client path calls this verifier for authenticated responses, and the storage-node server signs responses through the same scheme. Fresh sessions reset request IDs to 1 and increment predictably, making same-kind/same-id replay feasible if an attacker can observe and inject local RPC traffic within the 5 second freshness window. The finding is not remotely reachable through the public S3 HTTP surface by itself: storage RPC uses Unix-domain sockets whose parent directory must be private 0700. Therefore medium severity is appropriate: real confidentiality/integrity impact is plausible for local/internal compromise scenarios, but exploitation has significant preconditions and is not an internet-facing auth bypass.
## Controls
- Public S3 HTTP requests use application authentication/authorization outside this storage RPC path.
- Authenticated storage RPC requests and responses use signed control-plane envelopes.
- Storage RPC verifier enforces source, target, operation, topology, request_id, and timestamp freshness.
- Storage RPC freshness window is 5000 ms with 1000 ms allowed future skew.
- Storage-node RPC uses Unix-domain sockets, not a TCP listener.
- Storage-node socket parent directory must be private mode 0700.
- Storage RPC has transport frame size limits and admission/timeouts.
## Blindspots
- Static-only review cannot confirm actual production deployment topology, Unix socket ownership, or whether frontend/storage-node processes run under distinct users.
- No repository IaC or Kubernetes manifests were found/used to confirm ingress, load balancer, or host filesystem permission deployment details.
- The strongest validation evidence is a targeted unit-test PoC and debugger trace rather than a full end-to-end Unix-socket MITM exploit.
- If an attacker already has same-UID or root access to the host, they may have more direct access to storage files, reducing the incremental security impact of this replay flaw.
